{"format":"tvf-scene","v":1,"name":"example · What the geometry gives free","loop":60,"ease":"inout","hold":0,"gravity":1,"wind":0,"trail":null,"camera":[{"t":0,"box":[-0.810501266,-0.0857164557,-0.588486076,0.106450633]},{"t":0.16,"box":[-0.810501266,-0.0857164557,-0.588486076,0.106450633]},{"t":0.19,"box":[-0.529906329,-0.090843038,-0.342068354,0.0457336709]},{"t":0.33,"box":[-0.529906329,-0.090843038,-0.342068354,0.0457336709]},{"t":0.36,"box":[-0.272141772,-0.0830278481,0.0397367089,0.0784708861]},{"t":0.5,"box":[-0.272141772,-0.0830278481,0.0397367089,0.0784708861]},{"t":0.53,"box":[0.0438379747,-0.111091899,0.399326582,0.0951493671]},{"t":0.67,"box":[0.0438379747,-0.111091899,0.399326582,0.0951493671]},{"t":0.7,"box":[0.407935367,-0.0987988101,0.691242608,0.079192481]},{"t":0.84,"box":[0.407935367,-0.0987988101,0.691242608,0.079192481]},{"t":0.87,"box":[0.713164557,-0.0820253165,0.87721519,0.0410126582]}],"stops":[0.16,0.33,0.5,0.67,0.84],"beats":[{"title":"A dial","text":"A scrollbar lays a list on the writer's line, so [its thumb](#lit:1) shrinks as the list grows: for a billion rows, each pixel of the bar is a million rows wide. A dial lays the list on a turn. [Its hand](#lit:7) is pushed in ζ at a steady rate, stands at record x = sinh ζ, and is drawn at arctan x, inside [the quarter turn](#lit:2) however far it goes.\n\nWithin one window of home the records are [laid in proportion](#lit:3); past [the corner](#lit:5), one window out, [they come in ratios](#lit:4), two windows, four, eight, crowding toward the edge. Held at rate r, the hand reaches x after asinh(x)/r: a thousand records in 7.6/r, 2⁵³ in 37.4/r, a googol in 231/r. The list's length is never asked."},{"title":"The depth buffer","text":"A depth buffer stores (f/(f − n))·(1 − n/d), the in-place lay with its corner at [the near plane](#lit:14): setting the near plane is choosing the thumb. Of [the sixteen equal steps it stores](#lit:9), half land within twice the near plane and fifteen within sixteen times it, leaving one for the rest of the way to 10,000 n.\n\n[Log depth](#lit:11) spaces them evenly in ratio, the reader's lay. [The turn laid about a focus](#lit:13), F·tan(kπ/32), puts half each side of [the thing looked at](#lit:15), finest there. None was designed from the geometry; the geometry says where each put its corner."},{"title":"The flat screen","text":"Equal pixels on a plane are not equal turns. On [a cube's face of equal pixels](#lit:17), [a pixel at the middle of an edge](#lit:20) holds 0.35 of a middle pixel's directions, and a corner pixel 0.19, one part in 5.2.\n\nLaid by turn, an arctangent on each coordinate, the face still varies, but [its worst cell holds 0.71](#lit:21): a factor of √2. The flat screen's weighting, sec³α, is the design's, added to the geometry: three of the four cosines of a photograph's dark corners."},{"title":"What Math.floor throws away","text":"Math.floor(x / w) keeps [one cell](#lit:24) and drops where in it [x](#lit:23) was. The remainder is address, not data: its binary digits are the place halved, and halved again. Give each cell [its point](#lit:27,28,29,30,31,32), m + iγ, and [the cells of every depth](#lit:25) tile the hyperbolic plane, every tile of area 1. x = 0.8125 is 0.1101, cell 13 of 16, [reached one digit a step](#lit:26).\n\nThrough a reader at a height each depth [fades smoothly](#lit:36): half of a step is kept 3.09 depths coarser than its own cell, and it takes 5.5 depths to go from 90% kept to 10%. [The floor](#lit:35) keeps every coarser depth whole and none finer: a wall."},{"title":"Addresses on a turn","text":"[hash(key) % n](#lit:38) divides by a count. Add a machine, four to five, and 21 of these 24 keys move: [only three stay](#lit:39,40,41) where they were.\n\nPut keys and [machines](#lit:43,44,45,46) at angles on a hash's turn, each key served by the next machine round, and [a fifth machine](#lit:75) takes over [only 4 of the 24](#lit:67,68,69,70,71,72,73,74), those just before it. The angles carry no count; the machines are the payload painted on them."},{"title":"A steady state, and an origin","text":"A Kalman filter with process noise q and measurement noise r settles with [its two variances](#lit:80) at √(qr)·μ and √(qr)/μ, a reciprocal pair about [the noises' geometric mean](#lit:78). As [the noises](#lit:79) part, q/r from 1 to 1/16 to 16, μ goes 1.62, 1.13, 4.24, and the product stays qr: the corner, met by a recursion that knows nothing of corners.\n\nThe tests came out as ties and trades, never wins: the geometry names these schemes and adds none. What it adds is where each one put its corner, and the one amount each had to carry in. [The essay](https://github.com/reportbase/wander/blob/main/papers/essays/08-what-the-geometry-gives-free.md)"}],"path":null,"items":[{"fill":{"color":"#f4f1ea","opacity":1},"stroke":null,"width":0,"open":false,"exact":{"x":[-0.9,0.9,0.9,-0.9],"y":[0.216455696,0.216455696,-0.216455696,-0.216455696],"r":null,"smooth":null,"ctrl":null,"holes":[]}},{"morph":0},{"morph":1},{"morph":2},{"morph":3},{"morph":4},{"morph":5},{"morph":7},{"morph":6},{"morph":8},{"morph":9},{"morph":10},{"morph":11},{"morph":12},{"morph":13},{"morph":14},{"morph":15},{"morph":16},{"morph":17},{"morph":18},{"morph":19},{"morph":20},{"morph":21},{"morph":22},{"morph":24},{"morph":25},{"morph":23},{"morph":26},{"morph":27},{"morph":28},{"morph":29},{"morph":30},{"morph":31},{"morph":32},{"morph":33},{"morph":34},{"morph":35},{"morph":36},{"morph":37},{"morph":38},{"morph":39},{"morph":40},{"morph":41},{"morph":42},{"morph":43},{"morph":44},{"morph":45},{"morph":46},{"morph":75},{"morph":67},{"morph":47},{"morph":68},{"morph":48},{"morph":49},{"morph":50},{"morph":51},{"morph":52},{"morph":69},{"morph":53},{"morph":54},{"morph":55},{"morph":56},{"morph":57},{"morph":58},{"morph":59},{"morph":60},{"morph":61},{"morph":62},{"morph":63},{"morph":64},{"morph":65},{"morph":66},{"morph":70},{"morph":71},{"morph":72},{"morph":73},{"morph":74},{"